In this lesson, we'll look at direct proportion and rates, two ideas that show up everywhere once you know how to spot them. Two quantities are in direct proportion when doubling one doubles the other, so they grow together at a steady pace. The relationship can always be written as y equals k times x, where k is called the constant of proportionality, or just the unit rate. Think of it as how much y you get for every one of x. The recipe is simple. From any one known pair, compute k by dividing y by x. Then use that k to answer any other value, just multiply k by your new x to scale up, or divide y by k to scale down. Now, rates are really just these unit rates in disguise. A rate compares two quantities with different units, like kilometers per hour, dollars per kilogram, or liters per hundred kilometers. So when you see something like dollars per kilogram, that number is your k in the proportion formula. The trick is to read the units carefully and treat the rate as your constant. Now let's see it in action.